        th_line: 'Line', th_rule: 'Rule', th_sev: 'Severity', th_code: 'Code', th_fix: 'Suggestion',
        jwt_intro: 'Paste a JWT token to view its header and payload. Decoded in this browser only (not sent anywhere) and the <b>signature is not verified</b>, so do not trust the token contents based on this result alone.',
        jwt_clear: 'Clear', jwt_th_claim: 'Claim', jwt_th_value: 'Value', jwt_th_time: 'Time', jwt_th_status: 'Status', jwt_empty: '(empty)',
        dbg_title: 'Hook another tab (live, readable)',
        dbg_intro: 'Like Postman, it logs every fetch/XHR request as you use the target app normally — <b>works for plain and encrypted endpoints alike</b>. Each entry shows <b>method, URL, request headers, body, status, and response headers</b>; for endpoints with one-time per-request encryption (ephemeral ECDH) it also shows the <b>payload before encryption</b> (tapping <code>crypto.subtle.encrypt</code>) and the decrypted response (tapping <code>crypto.subtle.decrypt</code>) — useful because an old curl can\'t be reused (the key only lives briefly in tab memory). Since it records every call, this panel also works like a <b>crawler</b>: repeated requests to the <b>same method + URL are merged</b> into one entry with a <code>×N</code> counter (no duplicates), and the <b>export json</b> button downloads the whole unique list (link, method, headers, body, status, hit count) as a single JSON file.',
        dbg_step1: 'Open the target app tab, press F12 → <b>Console</b> tab, paste the script below and hit Enter.',
        dbg_step2: 'Or drag the <b>🪝 Hoox</b> button to your bookmarks bar once; after that just click it from the bookmarks bar while on the target tab, reusable many times without opening the Console.',
        dbg_step3: 'The panel appears at the bottom-right of the target tab. Every request is logged automatically; click ✕ in the panel to stop.',
        dbg_note: 'Contents are shown verbatim, untruncated. Everything stays in the target tab; nothing is sent to this scanner tab or any server.',
        rate_title: 'Rate Limiter test (from curl)',
        rate_intro: 'Like Postman, dedicated to checking a rate limiter. Paste the curl of one endpoint (e.g. login or search), set the count &amp; delay, then install the script in the target app tab — same idea as the Hook tab, so the request runs <i>same-origin</i> (session cookie included automatically, no CORS). The script repeats that request many times and reports the status/time/<code>Retry-After</code> header for each attempt. <b>Only use on systems you are authorized to test</b>; this sends real, repeated traffic.',
        rl_count: 'Request count', rl_delay: 'Delay between requests (ms)',
        rate_opt1_desc: 'Simplest, no need to paste a script into another tab. Works as long as the server allows CORS from this page\'s origin; otherwise use Option 2 below. The browser session cookie is <b>not</b> sent from here (different tab/origin) — good for endpoints authenticated via a header like an API key/Bearer token already in the curl.',
        rate_opt2_note: 'The panel in the target tab appears at the bottom-left (so it doesn\'t overlap the Hook panel at the bottom-right), with stop and copy-CSV buttons there too.',
        rate_limited: 'rate limited', failed: 'FAILED',
        rto_title: 'RTO test — slow request body',
        rto_intro: 'Tests whether the server enforces a <b>request read timeout</b>: the request body is dripped at the rate you set (e.g. 8 kbps). If the server waits too long for data, it closes the connection — that is the RTO being measured. This is <b>one slow connection for timeout diagnostics</b>, not flooding the server. A healthy server <i>should</i> cut off slow clients (see OWASP Slow HTTP / CWE-400 Uncontrolled Resource Consumption); if it doesn\'t, connections can be held open — a hardening finding. <b>Only use on systems you are authorized to test.</b>',
        rto_kbps: 'Send rate (kbps)', rto_size: 'Payload size (KB)', rto_repeat: 'Repeat', rto_delay: 'Delay between repeats (ms)',
